From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 04:30:48 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: report the codec resources to the framework

Android 17 measures what a device can still take: MediaCodec
.getGloballyAvailableResources() subtracts what every codec instance says
it needs from the capacity the component store reports. The v4l2 store
reported nothing, so the query failed and the availability tests that need
it could not run.

The store now answers the capacity and excluded resources, and each
encoder answers the resources it needs. There are two, both with vendor
ids: the codec sessions of the device, whose capacity is the concurrent
instance bound the components already enforce (added up over the codecs the
store offers), and the pixel rate, the luma samples per second of all
instances together, whose capacity is
ro.vendor.v4l2_codec2.video_pixel_rate, the product's measurement of what
the device sustains. An instance needs one session, and the pixel rate of
its stream when it runs in real time (priority 0) or none when it does not,
as it takes whatever the real-time instances leave. The operating rate
overrides the frame rate for the pixel rate. A product that sets no pixel
rate reports sessions only.

The resources follow the configuration: the component reports the new need
with the next output when the client switches between real time and not or
changes the rate, which is when Android expects its callback. The
parameters the store does not own still answer C2_BAD_INDEX for the whole
query; the helper that serves the resources reports them per field, and
the platform's dma-buf usage probe reads that as a store that has the
usage parameter.

The device serves the real-time streams of its instances only up to the
pixel rate it sustains. An instance whose start would take the sum past the
capacity is refused with C2_NO_MEMORY, which Android raises as
ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_RESOURCE, and the first instance is always admitted, as
the device serves one of whatever size it supports. A decoder joins by
reporting its need through videoResourcesNeeded() and claiming it from the
same budget.
